WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:03.219
Paying a fee to check luggage has become a
routine part of air travel

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:04.780
but it wasn't always this way.

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:07.780
In The United States, baggage fees started
in 2008,

00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:09.552
right around the time oil prices were peaking.

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:12.520
“Another day, another dollar"

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:14.460
“Crude oil broke another record"

00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:20.440
“We’re going to go from a profitable year
in '07 to a ten billion dollar loss in '08

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:23.180
and the only thing has changed is fuel.”

00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:25.880
"Jet fuel prices have them taking drastic measures..."

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:28.680
"Including a fifteen dollar charge for the first checked bag."

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:33.720
"Fifteen dollars? Holy cow! I'll have to put my underwear in my pockets!"

00:00:34.309 --> 00:00:38.149
To avoid the new fees, passengers started
cramming everything into their carry-ons.

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:41.500
But two years ago fuel prices dropped and
they’ve stayed low ever since.

00:00:41.510 --> 00:00:44.059
The fees, however, haven’t gone anywhere.

00:00:44.059 --> 00:00:48.370
The only exception among major carriers is
Southwest Airlines, which is famous for their

00:00:48.370 --> 00:00:50.630
“bags fly free” marketing campaign.

00:00:50.630 --> 00:00:52.730
"We duffel bags, golf bags, small bags..."

00:00:52.730 --> 00:00:55.250
"These bags right here, they fly free!"

00:00:55.250 --> 00:00:56.990
"I wouldn't pay to fly in here."

00:00:56.990 --> 00:00:58.990
"Why would these airlines charge for bags?"

00:00:58.990 --> 00:01:00.790
"Why do you charge for bags?!"

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:02.320
To make money.

00:01:02.329 --> 00:01:03.289
Lots of money.

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:07.400
Last year, revenue from baggage fees was nearly
4 billion dollars.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:11.160
And the 7.5% excise tax that airlines have
to pay on ticket prices

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:13.440
doesn’t apply to baggage fees.

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:16.900
So the tax code actually encourages the use
of fees.

00:01:16.909 --> 00:01:18.450
And they haven’t stopped at checked bags.

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:20.550
With carry-on space now increasingly scarce,

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:24.540
airlines can charge a fee to board early so you can claim spots in the overhead bin.

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:27.240
Revenue from the fees, combined with other

00:01:27.250 --> 00:01:30.479
factors like major mergers, have meant higher
profits for airlines.

00:01:30.479 --> 00:01:33.699
But what’s good for the airlines has not
benefited consumers...

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:37.399
Airlines haven’t lowered airfares in proportion
to higher profits.

00:01:37.399 --> 00:01:41.020
The planes are filling up anyway, so they
don’t need to compete on fares.

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:45.549
And all those carry-on bags are contributing
to longer lines at airport security.

00:01:45.549 --> 00:01:50.060
This summer, passengers were stuck at two,
three, and even four hour lines at airports

00:01:50.060 --> 00:01:51.380
across the country.

00:01:51.380 --> 00:01:55.320
In response, a group of senators and the head
of Homeland Security asked the airlines to

00:01:55.460 --> 00:01:56.180
waive baggage fees.

00:01:56.180 --> 00:02:01.440
"We've asked the airlines to consider possibly eliminating the checked baggage fees."

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:05.840
"There has been an increase in the carry-ons due to these fees."

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:08.720
So, isn’t it time to get rid of baggage
fees?

00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:11.200
Well...there’s more to the story.

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:15.710
A recent study has revealed a major benefit
to consumers that came from baggage fees:

00:02:15.710 --> 00:02:18.590
better on-time performance for all airlines.

00:02:18.590 --> 00:02:21.420
According to the research done by a group
of business school professors

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:26.480
charging fees to check two bags decreases the average departure delay by two minutes.

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:32.020
What they found is that the increase in luggage above the cabin has not off-set the reduction below the cabin.

00:02:32.530 --> 00:02:36.930
Although it seems like carry-ons make the
boarding process longer, down on the ground

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:38.650
the airlines are handling fewer bags.

00:02:38.650 --> 00:02:42.250
When you carry your own bags between connecting
flights, that’s work they don’t have to do

00:02:42.250 --> 00:02:44.710
and it seems to be saving time overall.

00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:47.760
That means fewer flight delays and fewer missed
connections.

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:51.090
There’s also been a decline in the number
of lost bags.

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:55.780
So, when it comes to baggage fees, it’s
not all bad for airline consumers.

00:02:55.780 --> 00:02:57.740
It’s just mostly bad.

